WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.600
The "John Wick'' franchise takes&nbsp;
place in a stylish reality that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.640
while captivating, doesn't&nbsp;
always make total sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.600
Here are a few questionable aspects of the "John&nbsp;
Wick" movies that many fans choose to ignore.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.560
How are there so many assassins in&nbsp;
one place at any given time? New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.280
in particular, appears to be infested with them.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.400
As the story of John Wick continues, it becomes&nbsp;
apparent that Wick isn't the only assassin around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.080
The Continental Hotel, for example,&nbsp;
is an entire boarding facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.680
dedicated to provide a safe space&nbsp;
for those in the business to relax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.520
have a drink, and restock on whatever&nbsp;
weapons and ammo they might need.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.480
When John is declared "excommunicado" by Winston,&nbsp;
the owner of the New York Continental, at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.440
of "John Wick: Chapter 2," every single person who&nbsp;
happens to be outside during the public meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.920
turns out to be part of the criminal underworld.&nbsp;
As Wick begins his hour-long head start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.640
many of the people he runs past read the&nbsp;
excommunicado notification on their cell phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.400
and look at him with solemn recognition.&nbsp;
It's a striking scene that shows how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.240
relatively powerless Wick is when compared&nbsp;
to the authority of the all-seeing High&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.720
Table — but don't think about it too hard,&nbsp;
or it might lose some of its movie magic.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.280
Why do we ignore this plethora of murderous&nbsp;
hit-people all congregating in the same public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.600
place? It does make sense that they'd all be&nbsp;
near the Continental, and that the various New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.240
York mobs would have enforcers stationed nearby&nbsp;
— but one hundred percent of the people there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.400
It doesn't make sense, even in the world&nbsp;
of Wick. With this many assassins around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.400
the underworld ceases being the underworld&nbsp;
— at this point, it's just the world.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.920
In the shadowy black markets&nbsp;
of the "John Wick" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.280
the Gold Coin is the currency of&nbsp;
choice. Wick's stash of gold buys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.640
him everything from a taxi ride for his dog&nbsp;
to corpse disposal. Medical attention, guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.760
a new suit — all of it costs a single coin&nbsp;
each and no one ever refuses it as payment.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.360
As explained by SYFY Wire, the&nbsp;
coins aren't just about payment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.280
but about a code that keeps everyone&nbsp;
working for the mythical world's High Table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.160
on the honor system. Instead of representing a&nbsp;
set amount of money, a coin represents a favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.520
that can then be redeemed for anything&nbsp;
else one might need — that is, unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.840
you're shopping at a normal store like a normal&nbsp;
person. Assassins need to eat too, after all.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.040
We know from the first film that Viggo Tarasov has&nbsp;
plenty of cash alongside his coins, and that other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.600
mob bosses have enough cash lying around that&nbsp;
it needs to be laundered. Additionally, bounties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.800
posted on the Operators' Board are offered not in&nbsp;
coins, but in cold hard cash. What makes something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.080
worth paying coins and not cash? If coins are the&nbsp;
currency that buys loyalty and silence, why offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.640
cash at all? Everyone has regular money in this&nbsp;
world — the trouble starts for John when he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.840
buying gas, after all — but the exact nature of&nbsp;
this underground economy doesn't exactly add up.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.120
In the scene where Viggo tells Iosef&nbsp;
exactly who and how dangerous John Wick is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.840
he refers to him as "Baba Yaga", as well&nbsp;
as "The Boogeyman." Or, more accurately,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.520
"He was the one you sent to&nbsp;
kill the f---ing Boogeyman."

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.640
The only problem is that John&nbsp;
Wick is nothing like Baba Yaga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.960
at least not the version from Russian folklore.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.520
According to the legend, Baba Yaga is an old&nbsp;
witch who eats children and lives in a house that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.000
walks on chicken legs. Yes, she and Wick are both&nbsp;
Russian, but that's about where the similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.560
end. Baba Yaga is a Russian "boogeyman,"&nbsp;
used to scare children into behaving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.280
but Wick is nothing like her if the fairy tales&nbsp;
are to be believed. It's a cool nickname, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
and sounds very intimidating — which is probably&nbsp;
why "Wick" fans are willing to look past it.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.280
One thing the "John Wick”&nbsp;
franchise has plenty of is mayhem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.520
While some of it occurs in dark rooms and&nbsp;
elaborate setpieces, much of it occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.320
in public spaces where most of the people&nbsp;
present are presumably innocent bystanders.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.760
During some of those public fights,&nbsp;
bystanders scream and scatter as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.680
soon as bullets start flying. In other&nbsp;
cases, though, no one seems to notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.840
the violence taking place right in front of&nbsp;
them. Probably the least plausible of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.720
fights is the final altercation between John&nbsp;
Wick and Cassian — the bodyguard of Gianna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.080
the woman John has been sent to&nbsp;
assassinate in "John Wick: Chapter 2."

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.920
John and Cassian take shots at each other&nbsp;
in a subway station while everyone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.640
continues to go about their business, seemingly&nbsp;
unaware that a firefight is going on. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.720
both men have silencers on their guns,&nbsp;
but silencers don't really work that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.560
John and Cassian's fight being ignored is a&nbsp;
little easier to forgive once they actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.640
get on the subway — after all, you know&nbsp;
what they say about New York subway riders.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.640
There's also the Grand Central Station fight&nbsp;
in "John Wick: Chapter 3 – Parabellum,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.360
where Zero and his henchmen go after John as&nbsp;
crowds of passersby go about their business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.640
and as bodies drop left and&nbsp;
right, no one bats an eye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.840
Yes, New Yorkers generally like to keep to&nbsp;
themselves — but frankly, this is ridiculous.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.320
John Wick is a fearsome killing machine,&nbsp;
known for his ability to take out entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.800
squads of combatants on his own. According&nbsp;
to Rotten Tomatoes, his body count throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.840
Chapters One to Three adds up to a whopping&nbsp;
299 — and that's just what we see onscreen.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.960
So why does everyone seem to like him so much?

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.240
It's not just "the code" that seems to&nbsp;
keep people from messing with Wick once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.440
he's retired and settled down. Everywhere he goes&nbsp;
in the first film, he commands respect. Charon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.920
the concierge at The Continental, and&nbsp;
Winston both seem very fond of him. Harry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.560
another assassin, helps him out, which costs&nbsp;
him his life. Aurelio clearly has deep respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.120
for him. In the sequels, the Bowery King&nbsp;
also seems to give Wick a lot of leeway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.400
despite their past interactions&nbsp;
being less than constructive.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.080
Is it the dogs? Though it's played as&nbsp;
a dark joke at times, Wick's return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.440
the life is fueled by the theft of his&nbsp;
car and the death of his dog — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.840
stings particularly bad since the dog was a&nbsp;
posthumous gift from his late wife, Helen.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.480
But this is John Wick, Baba Yaga, a man&nbsp;
who once killed three people with a pencil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.360
By all accounts, he's killed more people than&nbsp;
Freddy Krueger and Jason Voorhees combined.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.040
He's pretty damn scary if he's coming after&nbsp;
you, and you probably wouldn't be inclined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.960
to greet him with a smile. But he likes&nbsp;
dogs, so that makes him a heck of a guy.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.600
Another questionable thing "Wick" fans&nbsp;
ignore is how often Wick's opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.000
will give him a moment to compose himself&nbsp;
before they continue fighting to the death.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.800
The best example of this is the final boss fight&nbsp;
with Zero and his henchmen in "Parabellum."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.120
John manages to escape to the Continental&nbsp;
after being chased by Zero and his men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:02.080
and he appears to be safe. However, Winston&nbsp;
defies the High Table and The Continental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.720
is "deconsecrated," meaning once-forbidden&nbsp;
activities are now allowed on the grounds.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.440
Zero's martial artist henchmen fight with John&nbsp;
in the Continental. They are fresh for fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.280
while John is tired from all the relentless&nbsp;
attacks he's survived so far. They have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.000
clear advantage over him several times, yet they&nbsp;
continuously stop the fight to give John a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.480
to compose himself — ostensibly as a sign&nbsp;
of honor. They have a job to do, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.200
apparently so thrilled to fight the legendary&nbsp;
John Wick that they wind up losing instead.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.560
In most other films, John would've&nbsp;
been dead several times in that fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.080
and in many of the others he gets into&nbsp;
as well — but in the "John Wick" world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.760
like in a child's game of tag, "time&nbsp;
outs" are a natural part of combat.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.320
There's nothing like a good fight scene on&nbsp;
motorcycles, but anyone who knows anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.120
about bikes can't help but scoff at moments like&nbsp;
when John flips one cycle to take out another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.480
It's a cool-looking stunt, but it&nbsp;
also defies the laws of physics.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.960
When you look at the behind-the-scenes of stunts&nbsp;
such as the motorcycle chases in the third film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:02.160
the use of CGI is made quite apparent. Similarly,&nbsp;
Wick can fall off the roof of a skyscraper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.600
and survive thanks to the magic of&nbsp;
computers — as well as the stuntmen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.280
who get paid to do the dangerous&nbsp;
work Keanu Reeves doesn't do himself.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.800
Series director Chad Stahelski&nbsp;
is a former stuntman himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.800
so he knows full well what is physically&nbsp;
possible and what isn't. In a world as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.040
large and outrageous as that of "John Wick,"&nbsp;
Stahelski knows that only the biggest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.600
outlandish stunts will suffice. So, maybe&nbsp;
a motorcycle wouldn't fly backwards, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.840
would instead slide off to the side — but it sure&nbsp;
does look cooler when it happens in the movies.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.800
Sure, "John Wick " takes place in a highly&nbsp;
stylized fictional world where anything goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.600
but as its scale grows across borders and oceans,&nbsp;
it becomes increasingly difficult to believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.560
the machinations of the High Table have gone&nbsp;
completely unnoticed and unchecked by outsiders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.280
The films make it clear that at least one police&nbsp;
officer knows who John Wick is — a trope not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.480
uncommon for mob movies — but there is no sense&nbsp;
of exactly what an average bystander believes.

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.120
"Evening, John."

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.800
"Evening, Jimmy. Noise complaint?"

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:01.520
"Noise complaint."

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.160
"John Wick" features gruesome kills&nbsp;
in public spaces, huge explosions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.440
and even that one moment where Wick&nbsp;
rides a horse through the city — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.600
if everyone's trying to stay totally&nbsp;
unseen, they're not doing a very good job.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.240
The franchise posits that there is a code among&nbsp;
the assassins dictating that no bystanders are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.880
to be harmed, but there must be collateral damage&nbsp;
at some point — which would then come with cops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.200
investigations, and once the truth&nbsp;
is discovered, worldwide headlines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.600
Jimmy, the police officer who knows John, is&nbsp;
presumably on the payroll — so, is everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.640
else in the world as well? Is anyone glimpsed&nbsp;
on screen truly innocent? It's hard to tell.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.080
"Gas leak?"

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.120
"Yeah, gas leak."

00:08:42.634 --> 00:08:48.320
Iosef Tarasov is portrayed by Alfie Allen as an&nbsp;
indolent, spoiled brat. He's entitled, arrogant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.520
and unfortunately for him, not very bright. But&nbsp;
seriously, how does he not know who John Wick is?

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.160
Once Aurelio realizes the car Iosef has&nbsp;
brought to his chop shop belongs to Wick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:03.440
he rats him out to his father Viggo, the head&nbsp;
of the Tarasov crime family. Viggo has a talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.080
with his insubordinate son, explaining that John&nbsp;
Wick is more than just some assassin — he's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.920
most feared enforcer of all, and he was once on&nbsp;
Viggo's payroll. Iosef, in all his entitlement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.800
thinks he can just take out John himself — but he&nbsp;
has no idea of the wrath about to come his way.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.200
The thing is, everyone in the Wick world knows&nbsp;
who John Wick is. They know the gruesome stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.520
they know his nicknames, they know he got&nbsp;
out of the business for love. How does Iosef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.800
the son of Wick's former boss, not recognize the&nbsp;
man he took everything from, even after John spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.920
the same Russian dialect to him? Iosef's clearly&nbsp;
a major part of his father's business, even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.040
he's not the best "employee" in the organization.&nbsp;
Yes, Iosef is a dope, but it seems odd that he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.120
never even heard of the man who killed all of&nbsp;
his father's enemies in a single bloody night.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.920
The "John Wick" franchise is filled with&nbsp;
impossibilities, but one of the more questionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.640
ones involves John's long trek through the&nbsp;
desert in "John Wick: Chapter 3 – Parabellum."

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.000
After being declared excommunicado and finding&nbsp;
Berrada, the Master of Coin — Sofia's former boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.160
who is in charge of the Coin-making — John&nbsp;
learns that he cannot find the High Table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.120
but rather that they must come to him. He is&nbsp;
told to walk and walk until he can walk no more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:16.080
and if he's deemed worthy, the High Table will&nbsp;
scoop him up and allow him to speak to the Elder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.960
So, John walks alone for days without water&nbsp;
and collapses. He should be dead. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.520
nothing around him for miles, yet the High&nbsp;
Table finds him easily and picks him up.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.400
How does the High Table locate Wick? Do they&nbsp;
have invisible drones? Exceptionally powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.880
binoculars? Did they somehow hide a tracker on&nbsp;
him? For that matter, how does the High Table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:39.840
function at all? There are obviously switchboard&nbsp;
operators, the Elder, and the Adjudicator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.400
but how do they keep everyone in line? All of&nbsp;
these are questions that may never be answered.

